#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547116} :activity/id #uuid "540c8787-27dc-5427-9873-8e2c4e1e11ee" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b8c44-29d4-535a-ac99-52fb8107ea8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{4}-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546275} {:answer/id #uuid "9446f1cc-7e0b-53a2-b234-d433ab653901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{6})(s+\\frac{1}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546578} {:answer/id #uuid "a5049ad1-06bf-512f-9dc8-6aa664a5b508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{s}{4}-\\frac{1}{9})(s+\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546619} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1cc090-da2f-589a-a3e1-c6264a798d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{9})(s+\\frac{1}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546677} {:answer/id #uuid "d12fc8ca-be1b-524b-92aa-34847c9803af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546714} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189ffec-539c-5f18-adf5-a8178ffb8b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0e4d35de-1496-58dd-acc4-9d6da8717950" :question/math-academy-id "q-80009" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $\\frac{1}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}=\\frac{1}{4}({s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9})\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ {s}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{36}=\\frac{1}{4}(s-\\frac{1}{3})(s+\\frac{1}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0258decb-d7ba-5bf4-9551-9788a51c3985" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e25883-e605-54ce-9779-f65a82700d1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546330} {:answer/id #uuid "3dbe78df-90e1-5d3d-9a33-ac91b2965582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546387} {:answer/id #uuid "7792606e-6d16-5839-a489-c1736fe5432d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546519} {:answer/id #uuid "82617aa1-ef4b-5420-9570-d95b9a0b9144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546541} {:answer/id #uuid "915831fa-09ee-51d6-9ace-fef8f4f82b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546571} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2cd7973-c8ad-58ba-89e0-21d8e56fd509" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "895db0ca-e5a4-5d21-a8cf-89c7d272a0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-101899" :question/problem "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{x}{5}>2\\,?$\n\nI. $x=30$\n\nII. $x=15$\n\nIII. $x=10$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=30$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & >2 \\\\ \\frac{30}{5} & >2 \\\\ 6 & >2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=30$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=30$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=15$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & >2 \\\\ \\frac{15}{5} & >2 \\\\ 3 & >2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=15$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=15$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=10$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & >2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{5} & >2 \\\\ 2 & >2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=10$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=10$ **does not satisfy** this inequality."} :step/id #uuid "2162e5b3-3e3d-50a6-a556-bdd42418577f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c09a692-6cef-5a2d-8b5b-50519d26eebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{q}^{3}+4{q}^{2}+7q+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546271} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec1f128-3776-5271-8e30-9d0869bad2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{q}^{3}+8{q}^{2}+9q+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546350} {:answer/id #uuid "37dbcef1-e76b-5e55-a4db-d66c6d2410ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{q}^{3}+10{q}^{2}+12q+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546371} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ac5fc5-edea-5b7a-b307-204a859e6abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{q}^{3}+4{q}^{2}+3q+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546649} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbd346e-3a1c-5efc-8a0e-d27bc886ed8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{q}^{3}+2{q}^{2}+q+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546350} :answer-field/id #uuid "762de8d8-0159-59bb-8337-65093404743c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8e3a91e-2825-5391-a044-c55db4444bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-78166" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(q+1)({q}^{2}+7q+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute both terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q+1)({q}^{2}+7q+2) & = \\\\ q({q}^{2}+7q+2)+1({q}^{2}+7q+2) & = \\\\ {q}^{3}+7{q}^{2}+2q+{q}^{2}+7q+2 & = \\\\ {q}^{3}+8{q}^{2}+9q+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "30f74018-0e5e-5998-9921-62dfacf4dc96" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebb827d1-06d8-5d42-8723-79e381e48913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546783} :answer-field/id #uuid "51149079-3dcc-5052-8b68-1b2d681e947d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c7c751ec-42d9-5e30-a955-bbbf2b18ef36" :question/math-academy-id "q-175691" :question/problem "If $k=\\frac{21-3k}{4},$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{21-3k}{4} \\\\ \\frac{21-3k}{4} & =k \\\\ 4(\\frac{21-3k}{4}) & =4\\cdot k \\\\ \\cancel{4}(\\frac{21-3k}{\\cancel{4}}) & =4k \\\\ 21-3k & =4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21-3k & =4k \\\\ 21-3k+3k & =4k+3k \\\\ 21 & =7k \\\\ \\frac{21}{7} & =\\frac{7k}{7} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{\\cancel{7}k}{\\cancel{7}} \\\\ 3 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "49eae33d-37c8-532e-9c92-2ce045176d20" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547163}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d31ba6-6648-5d65-ad96-c543fe94436d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546506} {:answer/id #uuid "9a08680b-1b4d-5c5f-9be5-4353c5548985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "110%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546595} {:answer/id #uuid "a50805d3-2485-5b8b-9968-ce0b5efb4aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546620} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7e154f-44d3-5452-a2f5-f9e7ea6a2fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546739} {:answer/id #uuid "de9480d8-0c11-519a-b849-0edfe45b1259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "108%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546744} :answer-field/id #uuid "a847d1b0-a797-5a54-9b9a-b76dbf9897f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d087556b-949f-5317-94fd-7ed1bb1bb119" :question/math-academy-id "q-327578" :question/problem "Museum attendance increased to $120%$ of last year’s total. Of those visitors, $90%$ also attended the art exhibit. What percentage of last year’s total attended the art exhibit?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the number of visitors last year.\n\nWe're told that attendance increased to $120%$ of last year’s total. Thus, the new number of visitors is $1.2x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those visitors, $90%$ attended the art exhibit. So, the number of visitors who attended the art exhibit is\n\n$$\n0.9(1.2x)=1.08x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $1.08x$ represents $108%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $108%$ of last year’s total attended the art exhibit."} :step/id #uuid "4e40c7d9-9377-599c-9025-8f49a608188e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "456cdfd2-fb44-5da2-8f07-bd5c211a58c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $\\frac{4}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546406} {:answer/id #uuid "757d810c-9e86-5fbf-8cf5-913d44c8ecd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{4}{3}$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546511} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf9b410-474f-58b7-864c-a13646cfc872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{4}$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546600} {:answer/id #uuid "b6328fa1-753b-51ab-91b8-d3ce81c4b837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ or $\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546658} {:answer/id #uuid "e08e6d55-7d34-58f1-a2d9-caea99476030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{4}{3}$ or $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546511} :answer-field/id #uuid "261897d8-0c26-54e1-abb6-3fd7c538e5ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5cc362bd-9ac6-5268-99be-c1ee05140ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15255" :question/problem "If $|6x+1|=7,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|6x+1|=6x+1,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6x+1| & =7 \\\\ 6x+1 & =7 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{6x}{6} & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|6x+1|=-(6x+1),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|6x+1| & =7 \\\\ -(6x+1) & =7 \\\\ -6x-1 & =7 \\\\ -6x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{-6x}{-6} & =\\frac{8}{-6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $x=1.$"} :step/id #uuid "687d3552-6cdf-5129-a221-f64b5d491ce4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1741d705-b726-5f5c-90cb-ea350a8858b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546302} {:answer/id #uuid "17de54c2-e2f3-5bc4-aaf4-4390ce60e923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546303} {:answer/id #uuid "2564e473-7170-52c5-9ab1-e794dcf3497b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{x}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546334} {:answer/id #uuid "a70fb736-9b2d-58e1-941f-63021158e648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{x}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546625} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2b7d44-3d0a-5b72-903e-8924ed5d9e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546334} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d018f2b-9cf3-598c-a398-3c4cb351b41b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "85974f7c-3580-5a6c-8874-1948623d22ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-2683" :question/problem "${(2{x}^{2})}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2{x}^{2})}^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}{({x}^{2})}^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}{x}^{2\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ 16{x}^{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "992e51d2-63c8-5254-b80a-f62e089a6bb3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363c5b82-f408-5bb6-8aec-97014a4cfa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\cdot {3}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546367} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1cc211-24d7-53af-90b0-2c52e75869ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{15}\\cdot 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546562} {:answer/id #uuid "9eee6653-8c07-57a0-979e-2d483e2ca9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{12}\\cdot {3}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546606} {:answer/id #uuid "e9602b21-d8ad-5b5e-a17e-adee36d38bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546775} {:answer/id #uuid "f40ecd04-70b6-5a81-9df6-cb3a9c87912a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{4}^{15}\\cdot {3}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546799} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de8c48b-a34c-519d-bb8e-f7060eb81878" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7abf9e52-11fe-5f3b-b0b9-36cef1ac7576" :question/math-academy-id "q-100966" :question/problem "${4}^{8}\\cdot {(4\\cdot 3)}^{7}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n{4}^{8}\\cdot {(4\\cdot 3)}^{7}={4}^{8}\\cdot {4}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n{4}^{8}\\cdot {4}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{7}={4}^{8+7}\\cdot {3}^{7}={4}^{15}\\cdot {3}^{7}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "adc521f0-5c7e-5fc9-be81-2163319851a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a779e3c0-db96-5a84-bf30-5894050e9878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546626} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecbd6582-13a5-5dd4-8f0c-314a10cf5dbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7a7bc64-476c-516f-9ef3-e891c084febe" :question/math-academy-id "q-218571" :question/problem "If $81{a}^{2}=16,$ then $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the ${a}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{a}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{81}} \\\\ |a| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ |a| & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ a & =\\pm \\frac{4}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e172f160-857b-50ac-91c7-c14222212eab"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47eee787-3e3e-501f-9715-79d01e3c299b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546410} {:answer/id #uuid "76ee5ca0-7c12-5082-8a34-6cede44eec73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\sqrt[3]{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546518} {:answer/id #uuid "9